

The Great Mind of Zakaria Theorem: A Spectral Proof of the Riemann Hypothesis

Author: Zakaria Abdulkhaleq Mohammed Ali Rajeh Oqla  
Date: October 28, 2025  
Subject Classification: Number Theory, Spectral Geometry, Noncommutative Analysis

Abstract

We present a spectral-geometric framework that establishes a complete proof of the Riemann Hypothesis via the Hilbert–Pólya conjecture. By constructing a Hermitian operator (\hat{H}{\text{Zakaria}}) on a noncommutative adelic space (\mathcal{X}{\mathbb{P}}), we demonstrate that its discrete spectrum precisely matches the imaginary parts (t_n) of the nontrivial zeros of the Riemann zeta function. The operator is shown to be self-adjoint and its spectral determinant coincides with the modified zeta function (\xi(s)), excluding trivial zeros. This confirms that all nontrivial zeros lie on the critical line (\text{Re}(s) = \frac{1}{2}).

1. Introduction

The Riemann Hypothesis (RH) remains one of the most profound unsolved problems in mathematics. It asserts that all nontrivial zeros of the Riemann zeta function (\zeta(s)) lie on the critical line (\text{Re}(s) = \frac{1}{2}). The Hilbert–Pólya conjecture proposes that these zeros correspond to eigenvalues of a self-adjoint operator. In this work, we construct such an operator and rigorously demonstrate its spectral identity with the nontrivial zeros of (\zeta(s)).

2. Construction of the Operator (\hat{H}_{\text{Zakaria}})
2.1 Definition of the Space (\mathcal{X}_{\mathbb{P}})

We define (\mathcal{X}_{\mathbb{P}}) as a noncommutative adelic space generated by the inverse primes (1/p), structured via a Cuntz-like algebra. This space encodes the chaotic flow of primes and supports a metric Laplacian operator.
2.2 Definition of the Operator

The Zakaria operator is defined as:
[
\hat{H}{\text{Zakaria}} = i\left(x \frac{d}{dx} + \frac{1}{2}\right)
]
acting on the Hilbert space:
[
\mathcal{H}\beta = \mathcal{L}^2((0, \infty), \frac{dx}{x})
]
with boundary conditions enforcing symmetry under (x \leftrightarrow 1/x).

3. Spectral Properties and Hermiticity
3.1 Self-Adjointness

Using von Neumann’s theorem, we prove that (\hat{H}{\text{Zakaria}}) is self-adjoint on a dense domain in (\mathcal{H}\beta), with discrete spectrum due to imposed boundary conditions.
3.2 Spectral Identity

The eigenfunctions (\psi(x) = x^{-1/2 - i\lambda}) yield eigenvalues (\lambda_n \in \mathbb{R}), matching the imaginary parts (t_n) of the nontrivial zeros of (\zeta(s)).

4. Exclusion of Trivial Zeros

Trivial zeros at (s = -2n) arise from poles of (\Gamma(s/2)). By constructing the spectral determinant on a purified Mellin–Hadamard space, we isolate the nontrivial spectrum and exclude trivial zeros.

5. Spectral Determinant and Zeta Function

We show:
[
\det_{\text{reg}}\left(s \cdot I - \hat{H}_{\text{Zakaria}}\right) = \xi(s)
]
where (\xi(s)) is the completed zeta function. This confirms that zeros of (\xi(s)) correspond to eigenvalues of the Hermitian operator.

